--TEST--
`$uri->compareTo` normalizes both references per RFC 3986 § 6.2.2 and then compares components, while `parse` still reports what was written — spec § 12, `rule:classes/comparable`, `rule:expressions/object-identity-equality`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Uri::compareTo
// § 6.2.2.1 and § 6.2.2.2: the scheme and the host fold to lower case, and an
// escape that spells an unreserved character is that character. `parse` still
// reports what was written, which is what the second line pins.
var $written = Core\Uri::parse("HTTP://Example.COM/a%7Eb");
var $plain = Core\Uri::parse("http://example.com/a~b");
echo $written->compareTo($plain), "\n";
echo $written->toString(), "\n";

// § 6.2.2.3: dot segments are removed from an absolute path.
echo Core\Uri::parse("http://h/x/./y/../z")->compareTo(Core\Uri::parse("http://h/x/z")), "\n";

// The three lines § 6.2.2 deliberately does not cross. A default port and an
// empty path are § 6.2.3, which needs a table of schemes; an escape spelling a
// reserved octet stays escaped, so `%2F` is never a `/`; and only the scheme
// and the host are case-insensitive.
echo Core\Uri::parse("http://h:80/")->compareTo(Core\Uri::parse("http://h/")), "\n";
echo Core\Uri::parse("http://h/a%2Fb")->compareTo(Core\Uri::parse("http://h/a/b")), "\n";
echo Core\Uri::parse("http://h/A")->compareTo(Core\Uri::parse("http://h/a")), "\n";

// `rule:classes/comparable`: the answer is an order, not only an equality — component
// lexicographic, in the order the readers are declared.
echo Core\Uri::parse("http://a/")->compareTo(Core\Uri::parse("http://b/")), "\n";
echo Core\Uri::parse("http://b/")->compareTo(Core\Uri::parse("http://a/")), "\n";
--EXPECT--
0
HTTP://Example.COM/a%7Eb
0
1
-1
-1
-1
1
